---
import NeoMyco from '../../layouts/NeoMyco.astro';
import NeoTerm from '../../components/NeoTerm.astro';
import {basics,buttondownUsername,interests} from '../../data/neoMyco';
const practices=[['01','Read the habitat','Look beyond the cap. Notice nearby trees, the wood or soil beneath a mushroom, and the conditions that bring fungi into view.'],['02','Make a useful record','Practice photographs, field notes, and iNaturalist observations that give other people something to work with.'],['03','Keep the connections','Learn thoughtful collecting, labeling, and preservation where collecting is permitted. Keep each specimen connected to its observation.'],['04','Follow the evidence','Learn what microscopy and DNA sequencing can tell us, where they fall short, and which questions are worth asking next.']];
const pipeline=[['01','Find','Photograph each find in place and post it to iNaturalist with habitat notes.'],['02','Voucher','Dry and label the specimen so it stays linked to its observation.'],['03','Sequence','Send tissue for an ITS DNA barcode, the standard first sequence for fungi.'],['04','Put it on the record','Add the sequence to the observation and GenBank, where researchers can find and build on it.']];
const signupOpen=buttondownUsername.trim()!=='';
---
<NeoMyco>
<section class="hero wrap"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="kicker">A COMMUNITY TAKING ROOT · PLANNED FOR 2027</p><h1>There’s more here than meets <em>the eye.</em></h1><p class="lead">In familiar woods. Under the neighborhood oaks. In the questions we haven’t learned to ask yet.</p><p>NEO Myco is a new community taking shape in Northeast Ohio. We’re bringing people together to explore fungi, learn field mycology, and make observations that others can build on.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="#join">Get launch updates <span>↓</span></a><a class="text-link" href="#barcoding">What makes us different ↓</a></div><p class="margin-note">Bring your curiosity. We’ll learn together.</p></div><figure class="specimen hero-plate"><div class="plate-head"><span>FIELD RECORD / 001</span><span>SUMMIT COUNTY, OHIO</span></div><img src="https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/314765196/large.jpg" alt="Inocybe communis photographed in Donn Bialik’s front yard in 2023" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption><span class="hand-note">A discovery close to home.</span><h2><i>Inocybe communis</i></h2><div class="label-grid"><span>OBSERVED<strong>28 AUG 2023</strong></span><span>OBSERVER<strong>DONN BIALIK / MYCOGEEKY</strong></span></div><p>Two small mushrooms near mature white oaks. An observation later cited in a species description.</p><a href="#field-record">Follow this collection’s story ↓</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="principles wrap"><span>Explore</span><span>Learn</span><span>Document</span><span>Contribute</span></div>
<section class="wrap section basics" id="basics"><div class="section-heading"><p class="kicker">01 / THE BASICS</p><h2>What to know <br/><em>before 2027.</em></h2><p class="lead">The short version, for anyone wondering whether this is their kind of club. Details will be filled in as they are decided.</p></div><dl class="basics-grid">{basics.map(([k,v])=><div><dt>{k}</dt><dd class:list={{tba:/to be announced\.?$/i.test(v)}}>{v}</dd></div>)}</dl></section>
<section class="mission" id="barcoding"><div class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">02 / WHAT MAKES NEO MYCO DIFFERENT</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>A club where members’ <br/><em>collections get sequenced.</em></h2></div><p class="mission-statement">Our mission: sequence everything we find at every event, except <NeoTerm term="oversampled taxa" id="oversampled">Species that already have plenty of reliable DNA sequences in public databases, usually common and easy-to-recognize fungi. One more sequence of them adds little, so we spend sequencing on finds that can still teach us something.</NeoTerm>.</p><p class="lead">Most mushrooms found on a foray are never sequenced, so their names rest on photos and field marks alone. Members will learn every step, from the find to the public record, and we’ll help get standout collections sequenced.</p><ol class="pipeline">{pipeline.map(([n,title,body])=><li><span class="number">{n}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></li>)}</ol><p class="mission-bridge">Here’s what that path looked like for one front-yard find. <a href="#field-record">Follow the <i>Inocybe communis</i> record ↓</a></p></div></section>
<section class="case-section" id="field-record"><div class="wrap section"><p class="kicker">03 / A REAL EXAMPLE · FROM THE FOUNDER’S FIELDWORK</p><div class="section-heading"><h2>From the front yard <br/><em>to the scientific record.</em></h2><p class="lead">A familiar place can hold an unfamiliar species.</p></div><div class="case-layout"><figure class="specimen"><div class="plate-head"><span>OBSERVATION 180676766</span><span>28.08.2023</span></div><img src="https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/314765219/large.jpg" alt="Another view from Donn’s 2023 Inocybe communis collection" loading="lazy"/><figcaption><h3>A record worth keeping.</h3><p>Donn’s field notes place these mushrooms near three large white oaks (<i>Quercus alba</i>). The record links photographs, habitat notes, and an ITS DNA barcode.</p><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/180676766">Explore the original observation ↗</a></figcaption></figure><div class="record-story"><article><p class="kicker">I / NOTICE & DOCUMENT</p><h3>It started in the front yard.</h3><p>On August 28, 2023, Donn Bialik documented two small mushrooms at home. Careful records gave the find a life beyond the day it was collected.</p></article><article><p class="kicker">II / A WORKING IDENTITY</p><h3>A name with a question still attached.</h3><p>The observation’s history includes <i>Inocybe</i> sp. “radiata-IN01”: a provisional label used to keep track of a lineage while its identity was investigated. Its sequence is recorded in GenBank as <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/PP850762">PP850762 ↗</a>.</p></article><article><p class="kicker">III / SHARED KNOWLEDGE</p><h3><i>Inocybe communis.</i> Formally described.</h3><p>In 2026, Sigrid Jakob, Scott Ostuni, Kyle Canan, Linas V. Kudzma, and P. Brandon Matheny published descriptions of six new species of Inocybaceae, including this one.</p><p>Donn’s 2023 observation is cited among representative iNaturalist records with molecular annotations. The authors explicitly distinguish these records from specimens they examined directly.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00275514.2026.2685369">Read the research paper ↗</a></article></div></div><div class="return-record"><img src="https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/715327092/large.jpg" alt="Inocybe communis observed again on Donn’s property in August 2026" loading="lazy"/><div><p class="kicker">IV / RETURN TO THE SAME PLACE</p><h3>The story keeps growing.</h3><p>A second front-yard observation, made August 11, 2026, is also identified as <i>Inocybe communis</i> and has its own DNA barcode. Revisiting a familiar place gives us more opportunities to notice, compare, and ask questions.</p><a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/observations/390447468">See the 2026 observation ↗</a></div></div><p class="source-note">Photographs and observations: Donn Bialik (MycoGeeky). These records predate NEO Myco’s launch. Sigrid, Scott, and Kyle are friends of Donn; Scott and Sigrid are also involved in the Amateur Mycology Publishing Group. Their collaborative work helps inspire what we’re building.</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap section" id="in-the-field"><div class="section-heading"><p class="kicker">04 / THE COMMUNITY WE’RE BUILDING</p><h2>A walk in the woods. <br/><em>A place for your questions.</em></h2></div><div class="intro"><p class="lead">MycoView’s curiosity, put into practice. Outdoors, around a collection table, and alongside people who want to keep learning.</p><p>Our planned forays and workshops will connect the pleasure of finding mushrooms with ecology, careful collections, iNaturalist, microscopy, and DNA evidence. You can come for a richer afternoon in the woods, or follow a question further. Both belong here.</p></div><p class="practice-intro">Four everyday habits feed that path:</p><div class="practice-grid">{practices.map(([n,title,body])=><article><span class="number">{n}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div><aside class="note"><strong>A next step, within reach.</strong><p>We’re developing practical training and opportunities to learn alongside more experienced naturalists. Bring an interest, a skill, or a question—and help shape what we explore together.</p></aside></section>
<section class="wrap section woods"><div><p class="kicker">05 / KNOW YOUR WOODS</p><h2>One place. <br/>Many visits. <br/><em>More to discover.</em></h2></div><div><p class="lead">What fungi live in a place you already know?</p><p>Know Your Woods is our invitation to keep a fungal inventory of your own yard or a nearby trail: one place, visited again and again, until it starts to tell you things. It’s the everyday version of our mission, and you can start before the club launches.</p><a class="button" href="/neo-myco/know-your-woods/">Why a home or trail inventory? <span>→</span></a></div></section>
<section class="join wrap" id="join"><p class="kicker">THE NEXT PAGE IS STILL UNWRITTEN</p><h2>There’s room for <br/><em>your curiosity here.</em></h2><p>We’re preparing for a 2027 launch. Get an email when dates, forays and workshops are announced, and tell us what you’d like to do.</p>
<form class="signup" method="post" action={signupOpen?`https://buttondown.com/api/emails/embed-subscribe/${buttondownUsername}`:undefined} aria-describedby="signup-note">
<div class="signup-fields"><label><span>Email <span class="req">(required)</span></span><input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" required disabled={!signupOpen}/></label><label>Name<input type="text" name="metadata__name" autocomplete="name" disabled={!signupOpen}/></label><label>Town or county<input type="text" name="metadata__area" autocomplete="address-level2" disabled={!signupOpen}/></label></div>
<fieldset disabled={!signupOpen}><legend>I’m interested in</legend><div class="signup-interests">{interests.map(([tag,label])=><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="tag" value={tag}/>{label}</label>)}</div></fieldset>
<label class="signup-heard"><span>How did you hear about NEO Myco? <span class="opt">(optional)</span></span><input type="text" name="metadata__heard" disabled={!signupOpen}/></label>
<input type="hidden" name="tag" value="neo-myco"/><input type="hidden" name="embed" value="1"/>
<button class="button" type="submit" disabled={!signupOpen}>Get launch updates <span>→</span></button>
<p class="signup-note" id="signup-note">{signupOpen?'NEO Myco news comes in the MycoView newsletter: one email for both, a few times a year. Unsubscribe anytime. Sign-ups are handled by Buttondown.':'Sign-up opens shortly. Until then, message us on iNaturalist.'}</p>
</form>
<p class="join-alt">Prefer to talk first? <a href="https://www.inaturalist.org/people/mycogeekyohio">Message us on iNaturalist ↗</a></p><small>Dates and program details will be shared as they are confirmed.</small></section>
</NeoMyco>
